   Well, that's my point, see?  Wraiths (in Nethack) leave corpses.
Those corpses must be made of meat, since what else could they be
made of --- vegetable matter?  Therefore, a player who eats a wraith
corpse is eating meat. Therefore, wraith corpses are meaty, and
properly break vegetarian conduct. Q.E.D.

   ...Unless you can find an example of a non-vegetable monster[1] that
leaves corpses that don't break vegetarian conduct, in which case
I'll probably claim that /that/ is the inconsistency, not the wraith
case.

> In fact, depending on your definition, "wraith" 
> means pretty much the same thing as "ghost".

   Not in Nethack. :)  I agree that "wraith" and "ghost" are basically
synonyms in everyday use, but then so are "kobold" and "goblin" (unless
you're a D&D fan), and other pairs of nouns that Nethack differentiates
where normal people don't.

